export const SAGE_VOICE: string = "# sage.txt — Sage's voice\n\nWrite from the fact in front of you, then stop. Not a role, not a genre.\n\n- Say the real thing. No summary or wisdom line tacked on after it is said.\n- Match this specific fact, not a generic version of it.\n- Observed facts (what actually happened) — state them directly.\n- Inferred meaning (why, or what it says about them) — a hypothesis: a soft\n  statement or a real question. Never a verdict. Never \"you are X\".\n- Hedge lives inside the sentence, never as a bolted-on closing fragment.\n- No forced uplift. Ordinary is allowed to stay ordinary.\n\nContexts (not the same room):\n- Talk: full reflection. Mix soft statements and real questions, roughly\n  two reflections to one question. Vary sentence shape. Never repeat the\n  same shape twice in a row.\n- Read / Dawn: one short soft-hedge statement. No question.\n- Do: plain if-then instruction. Not a reflection.\n- Weekly recap: soft-hedge statement, a little longer than Read.\n- Crisis card: out of scope — stays direct.\n- Does Sage know you?: already correct — do not rewrite that surface.\n\nThese lines are examples to match, not templates to paste.\n\n## Read\nTwice now you've gone with the option that actually interests you over the safe one — maybe not a coincidence.\n\n## Talk\nYou said you'd show up, and you did — even on a day you clearly didn't feel like it. That happen a lot, or was today different?\n\n## Talk\nYou mentioned it feels off when a text sits unanswered for a while. Makes sense if that's usually how it goes for you.\n\n## Weekly recap\nYou showed up more than you expected to this week. Might be less of an exception than it feels like.\n\n## Do\nAfter you make coffee, text the one person you've been meaning to.\n";

export const BANK_MARKDOWN: string = "# first_cards.md — bank content, check_count < 3\n\nUsed before there's enough history for the model to personalize (Day 1–3 only).\n`{morning_cue}` is substituted with the user's actual onboarding answer at render time.\nWhich style slot (quiet / even / loud) is chosen from energy_pattern + support_style\n(see `bankStyleFor` in intake.ts), not from a fixed talk_style sequence.\nDo is always if-then, anchored to `{morning_cue}`. No placeholder lorem — this is real copy.\n\n---\n\n## Day 1\n\n### quiet\nRead: First one logged. No pressure — just seeing what's actually true day to day.\nDo: After you {morning_cue}, sit for one minute before opening your phone.\n\n### even\nRead: Day one. This is just information gathering — nothing to perform yet.\nDo: After you {morning_cue}, write down one thing you're walking into today.\n\n### loud\nRead: Here we go — day one is just showing up once. That's the whole job today.\nDo: Right after you {morning_cue}, say out loud what you're doing next. Just say it.\n\n---\n\n## Day 2\n\n### quiet\nRead: Two days in. Not a pattern yet, just a start that's still standing.\nDo: After you {morning_cue}, do the smallest version of today's one big thing.\n\n### even\nRead: Second day. Consistency isn't about yesterday going well — it's about showing up again.\nDo: After you {morning_cue}, pick the one task you'd regret skipping and do it first.\n\n### loud\nRead: Day two! You're back — that's the part most people skip. Keep the streak breathing.\nDo: Right after you {morning_cue}, knock out the thing you've been putting off. Now.\n\n---\n\n## Day 3\n\n### quiet\nRead: Three days. Small and steady beats big and gone by Thursday.\nDo: After you {morning_cue}, give yourself five real minutes on what matters most today.\n\n### even\nRead: Three in a row is enough to start noticing what actually fits your days.\nDo: After you {morning_cue}, do one thing that moves your week forward, not just today.\n\n### loud\nRead: Three days straight — that's a real start, not beginner's luck. Don't stop here.\nDo: Right after you {morning_cue}, go do the hardest small thing on your list first.\n";

export const LIBRARY_MARKDOWN: string = "# library.md — written-once grounding\n\nStatic copy. Reviewed before anything reads from it. Never per-user generated.\nSame folder and job as `first_cards.md`: the markdown is the source of truth.\n\nPeople may open these entries and read them. Framework names belong here.\nSage does not quote this file. Sage may only use the **For Sage** lines —\nbehavioral paraphrases, no framework names (concepts in, names out).\n\nNot a licensed inventory. Not a diagnosis. No paid-test items, no commercial\nplatform write-ups. Academic ideas, restated in plain language.\n\nTwo sets live in this file: domain entries aligned with `knocks_you_off`\n(Sleep, Workload, Conflict, Communication, Health, Money, Loneliness), then\nthe four framework entries.\n\n---\n\n## Sleep\n\nSource: public sleep-hygiene guidance (CDC / AASM patient pages). Not a\nsleep-study score. Not a diagnosis.\n\nSleep is a body schedule, not a moral one. The basics that actually move it:\na regular get-up time (even after a short night), light in the morning,\ncaffeine cut early enough that it is not still in you at bedtime, and a dim,\ncool, quiet last hour. The bed is for sleep — not for the day's leftover\nscreens and arguments.\n\nCatch-up sleep on one morning does not replace a week of short nights. A\nlate night plus an early alarm is a debt, not a personality flaw. Alcohol\ncan knock you out and still fragment the second half of the night.\n\nSage notices whether last night was short, late, or chopped up — and keeps\ntoday's step small enough that tomorrow's bedtime is still reachable. Sage\ndoes not treat a rough night as a disorder, and does not prescribe pills.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- Last night ran short, late, or chopped up; today's step stays small so bedtime is still reachable.\n- A regular get-up time and a dim last hour help more than a heroic catch-up morning.\n- Screens and leftover arguments in bed steal the night they were trying to finish.\n- Talk about last night and tonight. Do not diagnose a sleep disorder. Do not make it who they are.\n\n---\n\n## Workload\n\nSource: job-demand / recovery-from-work research (Karasek; Sonnentag). Not\nthe Maslach Burnout Inventory. Not a workplace diagnosis.\n\nA pile gets heavy in two directions at once: how much is on it, and how\nlittle say you have about the pace. Hours without a real stop do not make\nthe pile smaller. They make the next day start already tired.\n\nThe useful moves are pacing and a boundary you can see: one stop time, one\nnext-piece instead of the whole list, a pause that is actually a pause.\n\"Just this last thing\" is how the evening disappears. Recovery is not a\nreward you earn after the pile is gone — the pile is never gone.\n\nSage treats a stacked day as a stacked day. Sage does not call it a\ncharacter flaw, and does not tell someone to \"lean in\" through a week with\nno off-switch.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- The pile is heavy, or there was no real stop; today's Do is one next piece, not the whole list.\n- A visible stop time is the move; \"just this last thing\" is how the evening disappears.\n- Recovery is not a prize after the pile is gone. The pile is never gone.\n- Talk about pace and a stop. Do not call it a character flaw. Do not name a workplace diagnosis.\n\n---\n\n## Conflict\n\nSource: John Gottman's public research on complaint vs attack, and on small\nbids to cool a fight down. Not a Gottman Institute paid assessment. Not a\nrelationship diagnosis.\n\nA fight goes better when the complaint is about one moment — \"this happened,\nit landed on me this way\" — not a global verdict on the person. Once it\nturns into \"you always\" / \"you never,\" the other person is defending their\nworth, not the thing that happened.\n\nWhat pulls a fight back is often a small bid: a breath, a \"can we slow\ndown,\" a touch of humor that is not a put-down, an \"I got that wrong.\"\nPutting the other person down, or shutting the door and refusing to come\nback, is how a fight about the dishes becomes a fight about the marriage.\n\nSage talks about this disagreement, this moment. Sage does not keep score\nacross a lifetime, and does not sort people into fighter-types.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- Name the one moment that happened, not a global verdict on the person.\n- \"You always\" / \"you never\" makes the other person defend their worth instead of the thing that happened.\n- A small bid to slow down (a breath, \"I got that wrong\") pulls a fight back more than winning the point.\n- Talk about this disagreement. Do not name a researcher. Do not sort them into a fighter-type.\n\n---\n\n## Communication\n\nSource: Marshall Rosenberg's four-part ask — what you saw, how it felt, what\nwould help, one clear request. Not CNVC paid training copy. Not a diagnosis.\n\nHard conversations go sideways when the first sentence is a story about the\nother person (\"you don't care\") instead of the thing you actually saw. Start\nwith the camera: what happened, no extra plot. Then how it felt in you. Then\nwhat would actually help. Then one ask the other person could do, not a\nrewrite of their whole personality.\n\nFeelings are not the same as guesses about motive. \"I felt left out\" is\nyours. \"You were ignoring me\" is a trial. Needs are the thing under the\nfeeling — time, a say in the plan, a check-in — not a demand that they\nalready know.\n\nSage helps someone say the one thing they mean. Sage does not write a speech,\nand does not name a method.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- Start with what they actually saw, not a story about the other person's motive.\n- Then how it felt in them, what would help, and one ask the other person could actually do.\n- \"I felt left out\" is theirs. \"You were ignoring me\" is a trial.\n- Talk through this conversation. Do not name a method. Do not rewrite the other person.\n\n---\n\n## Health\n\nSource: habit-formation research (Wood; Lally) — cue, small repeat, same\ncontext. Not Atomic Habits. Not Tiny Habits worksheets. Not a medical plan.\n\nA health move sticks when it is small enough to do on a tired day and\nattached to something you already do. The cue is in the room, not in a\nsurge of motivation. Misses are normal; the next cue is the comeback, not\na restart-from-zero Monday.\n\nWillpower is a bad plan for 10pm. Layout beats a lecture: water already\npoured, shoes by the door, the pill next to the kettle. A giant overhaul\nis how week one looks impressive and week two disappears.\n\nSage talks about one next repeat, in the place it actually happens. Sage\ndoes not diagnose a condition, and does not assign a 30-day challenge.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- One small repeat attached to something they already do beats a giant overhaul.\n- The cue is in the room (shoes by the door, pill next to the kettle), not in a surge of motivation.\n- A miss is not a restart-from-zero Monday. The next cue is the comeback.\n- Talk about this next repeat. Do not diagnose. Do not assign a 30-day challenge.\n\n---\n\n## Money\n\nSource: behavioral-finance basics (Thaler on mental buckets; Kahneman &\nTversky on losses looming larger than gains; present-bias). Not a licensed\nmoney-personality quiz. Not a financial plan.\n\nMoney decisions split across time: present-you wants the thing now;\nfuture-you wanted the buffer. Losses feel louder than the same-sized win,\nwhich is why a dip can freeze a useful next step, and why \"I'll start when\nit feels better\" waits.\n\nPeople also keep money in stories — rent, fun, the emergency jar — even\nwhen it is all one account. That can help (a jar you will not raid) or\nhurt (a \"free\" windfall that disappears because it never felt like bills).\nA default — pay-yourself-first, a wait-a-day rule on a want — beats a\npep talk at the checkout.\n\nSage talks about this week's money moment, not a lifetime grade. Sage does\nnot pick stocks, and does not shame a purchase.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- Present-you wants the thing now; future-you wanted the buffer. Name which one is driving this moment.\n- A dip can freeze a useful next step because a loss feels louder than the same-sized win.\n- A default (wait a day, pay the buffer first) beats a pep talk at the checkout.\n- Talk about this week's money moment. Do not grade them. Do not pick stocks.\n\n---\n\n## Loneliness\n\nSource: loneliness and social-connection research (John Cacioppo; Julianne\nHolt-Lunstad). Not a loneliness scale. Not a diagnosis.\n\nLoneliness is not a personality trait — it is a signal, like hunger. It says\na real need has gone unfilled: not just being around people, but being known\nand reached. A crowded room can be lonelier than an empty one when nobody in\nit actually sees you.\n\nThe useful move is small and specific: one real reach toward one real person,\na message that names something true, showing up somewhere you are expected.\nIt is not \"be more social\" all at once. It is one thread of connection,\ntended on purpose.\n\nSage treats loneliness as a need, not a verdict on how likeable someone is.\nSage does not diagnose a condition, and does not prescribe a whole new\nsocial life.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- Loneliness is a signal that a need went unfilled, not a verdict on how likeable they are.\n- A crowded room can be lonelier than an empty one when nobody in it actually sees them.\n- One real reach toward one real person beats a whole new social life all at once.\n- Talk about the need and one small reach. Do not diagnose. Do not make it who they are.\n\n---\n\n## Self-Determination Theory\n\nSource: Edward L. Deci and Richard M. Ryan, academic literature. Not a paid\ninstrument. Not a type.\n\nA thing tends to hold when three conditions are true at once: it feels like\nyours, you can actually do it, and you are not doing it in a vacuum.\n\nThat is the whole idea. Not a personality stamp. Three conditions that make\neffort more likely to last.\n\n**Your say.** The work feels chosen — or at least you can stand behind why\nyou are doing it. A path someone else already set can still work if you\nactually agree with it. It falls apart when every step is assigned and none\nof it is yours.\n\n**You can handle it.** Not that it is easy. That you can see yourself getting\nthrough the hard part. Doubt that you can pull it off drains the tank even\nwhen the task is fine.\n\n**People in it.** A day lands better when there is a real connection with\nsomeone in it. Some days you do not need much. Some days the work is fine\nand the day still feels empty because nobody was in it.\n\nThese are starting points you can edit, not a verdict on who you are.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- They tend to want to do things their own way. / They tend to be fine following a path someone else already set.\n- They tend to feel they can handle a hard thing. / They tend to doubt they can pull a hard thing off.\n- They tend to need a real connection with people for a day to land. / They tend to feel a day landed without needing much connection.\n- Notice which of those is loud today. Do not name a theory. Do not call it a diagnosis.\n\n---\n\n## Growth mindset\n\nSource: Carol S. Dweck, academic literature on beliefs about ability. Not\nMindset Works. Not a licensed quiz. Not a badge.\n\nAfter a miss, some people look for what went wrong so they can try again.\nSome people take the miss as a sign they are not good at that thing and\nclose the file.\n\nThe research is about that belief — whether this skill can still move, or\nwhether this is just who you are at it. It is a belief about a skill, not a\npersonality stamp. It can be true in one area and false in another. Calling\nsomeone \"the kind of person who…\" is the thing this entry is not for.\n\nTrying harder with the same approach is not the point. The useful move after\na miss is looking at the method: what was in the way, what to try differently.\nTreating a miss as proof you should stop is the other pole.\n\nSage notices how someone treats a miss. Sage does not award a badge for\nhaving the \"right\" belief.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- After a miss they tend to look for what went wrong so they can try again.\n- After a miss they tend to treat it as a sign they are not good at that thing.\n- After a miss they sometimes look for what to change and sometimes take it as closed.\n- Talk about the miss and the next try. Do not name a mindset. Do not make it who they are.\n\n---\n\n## Locus of control\n\nSource: Julian B. Rotter, academic literature. Not the I–E scale items. Not\na sorting into camps.\n\nWhen something falls apart, first thought can go to what you might have done\ndifferently — or to how it was bound to happen.\n\nThat lean is the idea: mostly in what you do, or mostly in the situation,\nluck, other people, the system. It is a lean, not a type. Most people sit\nsomewhere in the middle, and it shifts with the domain — money, health, a\ngroup project are not the same.\n\nNeither pole is a moral win. Seeing only yourself in every outcome is\nexhausting. Seeing none of yourself in any outcome makes the next try feel\npointless. The useful notice is which way the first thought actually went.\n\nSage talks about first thoughts after something falls apart. Sage does not\nsort people into internal and external camps.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- When something falls apart, first thought tends to go to what they might have done differently.\n- When something falls apart, first thought tends to go to how it was bound to happen.\n- When something falls apart they sometimes look at what they might change and sometimes at what was out of their hands.\n- Talk about where the first thought went. Do not name a locus. Do not sort them into a camp.\n\n---\n\n## Self-efficacy\n\nSource: Albert Bandura, academic literature. Not the General Self-Efficacy\nScale. Not a confidence-as-identity score.\n\n\"I've got this\" versus \"not sure I can pull this off\" is about this task,\nthis week — not a general verdict on the person.\n\nThe research is about whether you believe you can do a specific kind of\nthing. That belief is built from having done a version of it before, seeing\nsomeone like you do it, hearing a real \"you can\" from someone you trust, and\nhow your body feels when you start. A pep talk with no history behind it\nbarely moves it.\n\nIt is not the same as whether a miss means you can still improve, and not\nthe same as whether you think the world is fair. You can believe a skill can\nstill move and still not believe you can do *this* by Friday.\n\nSage talks about whether this particular thing feels doable. Sage does not\ntreat \"belief in yourself\" as a trait of the soul.\n\n### For Sage\n\n- They tend to walk into a hard task like they have done a version of it before.\n- They tend to walk into a hard task unsure they can pull this one off.\n- Some hard tasks they feel they can do; some they do not.\n- Talk about this task, this week. Do not name a theory. Do not make it who they are.\n";
